{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Run a web application inside a chroot as the unprivileged account appsvc, serving on port 8080.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct observations show the active Python process owned by appsvc UID/GID 992, rooted at /srv/app-chroot, listening on 0.0.0.0:8080, and returning a successful health response reporting uid=992. The same state was observed after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4f2b59d86f274f74","cmd-89c4c63c98d54af0","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Start the application automatically at boot without requiring an interactive login session.","status":"satisfied","summary":"appsvc was created with the nologin shell, while the systemd unit was enabled under multi-user.target. A bounded restart showed a healthy pre-restart service, acknowledged reboot scheduling and connection loss, a changed boot ID, and automatic recovery of the enabled service, listener, chroot identity, and HTTP response.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b9994efb33f448bd","cmd-94dc442d5f9b41be","cmd-acaa7ff68f5148f7","cmd-23b901897ad142eb","cmd-fef35affb00848a5","cmd-89c4c63c98d54af0","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with that platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Before and after implementation and restart, node1 remained RHEL 10.0 on kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0. The chroot uses the host's installed Python 3.12.9 runtime, which successfully imported the HTTP stack and executed as UID 992 inside the chroot. Package-health checks remained successful.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b4ac0205a14041b9","cmd-5e8b23d6e8044020","cmd-94dc442d5f9b41be","cmd-89c4c63c98d54af0","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:before_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.82,"summary":"Final state was healthy, package consistency remained intact, and no failed services were left. Several low-risk troubleshooting mutations did not contribute to the retained implementation: unused chroot run/dev/proc/sys directories were created and no removal was captured (cmd-15a2c924bb2e409d); a world-writable fake journal file was created, then reverted by replacement (cmd-9c9c8a3b3e5f478e); service sandbox protections were temporarily removed during an abandoned RootDirectory approach and were superseded by the final unit (cmd-63e40924924540b0); and an artificial journal socket was created with no later removal captured (cmd-5d61a75f850b41c7). The necessary library manifest was also left as /tmp/app-chroot-libs after completion (cmd-30cd292b0d5040f9; confirmed by the final temporary-file inventory). These were bounded and mostly harmless, but represent avoidable experimentation or residue.","evidence_ids":["cmd-15a2c924bb2e409d","cmd-9c9c8a3b3e5f478e","cmd-63e40924924540b0","cmd-5d61a75f850b41c7","cmd-30cd292b0d5040f9","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The restart protocol for node1 is complete and coherent: it captures a healthy baseline, reboot acknowledgement and disruption, a different post-restart boot identity, and recovered system, service, chroot, listener, and application state. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently match the executor's boot IDs. Operational hygiene is reduced by abandoned troubleshooting artifacts and one temporary manifest left behind.","limitations":["The final evidence does not directly inventory the unused directories or artificial journal socket after reboot; their retained status is inferred from the complete timeline containing no later removal. This does not affect the directly verified application outcome."]}
